The last approach replaces the homomorphic multiplication with the MAC key \(\alpha\) on ciphertexts with the more efficient multiplication operation of the HSS by Boyle et al. [1]. More precisely, during seed generation secret shares of the MAC key \(\alpha\) times the secret key \(\mathsf{sk}\) are generated (instead of an encryption of \(\alpha\)). Then, the last level of multiplication (i.e. to obtain \(a\alpha,b\alpha,ab\alpha\)) can be replaced by a distributed decryption with the shares of \(\alpha\cdot\mathsf{sk}\), saving 3 homomorphic multiplications per triple generated. Note that this construction is compatible with the 'iterative' and 'packed' versions of BGV described. It does not seem competitive to use an HSS solely based on [1], as, when handling more than one homomorphic multiplication, their scheme has to account for the plaintext magnitude, which in the described setting would lead to significantly larger parameters.

## 8 Multi-Party PCG for Bilinear Correlations

In this section, we construct _multi-party_ PCGs for a useful class of bilinear correlations, capturing \(M\)-party OT, \(M\)-party vector OLE, \(M\)-party Beaver triples, and more. Incorporating into appropriate existing secure computation protocols, this yields secure \(M\)-party protocols for corresponding computations, with short correlated randomness, whose online execution requires only lightweight information theoretic operations and communication that scales _linearly_ in the number of parties \(M\).

Our construction approach provides a semi-generic transformation from any PCG for the corresponding 2-party bilinear correlation that satisfies an additional "programmability" property. Roughly, this property requires a way of "reusing" inputs across instances without compromising security. The \(M\)-party construction will leverage this structure by executing \(M(M-1)\) pairwise instances of the underlying 2-party PCG, for all the "cross-terms."

We obtain \(M\)-party PCGs for various bilinear correlations by identifying corresponding 2-party PCG constructions that satisfy the required programmability notion. In particular:

* \(M\)-party VOLE: From lightweight DPF and LPN, leveraging the 2-party VOLE generator of [1].
* \(M\)-party OT / Beaver triple: From group-based or lattice-based HSS, leveraging our 2-party PCGs from the previous sections.

Interestingly, the lightweight 2-party OT PCG from Section 5 does not seem to support programmability in the necessary manner, since the resulting sender message pairs are implicitly defined as a function of the receiver's bit selections.

We begin by defining the class of bilinear correlations, and the PGC programmability property to which our transformation applies.

Definition 37 (Simple Bilinear Correlation: 2-party): A \(2\)-party correlation \(\mathcal{C}\) is a _simple bilinear relation_ if there exists Abelian groups \(\mathbb{G}_{1},\mathbb{G}_{2},\mathbb{G}_{T}\) and a bilinear map \(e\colon\mathbb{G}_{1}\times\mathbb{G}_{2}\to\mathbb{G}_{T}\) for which \(\mathcal{C}\) is a distribution over \((\mathbb{G}_{1},\mathbb{G}_{T})\times(\mathbb{G}_{2}\times\mathbb{G}_{T})\) of the form

\[\mathcal{C}=\left\{((a,c),(b,d))\mid a\leftarrow\mathbb{G}_{1},b\leftarrow \mathbb{G}_{2},c\leftarrow\mathbb{G}_{T},d=e(a,b)+c\right\}.\]

Note that the groups \(\mathbb{G}\) and map \(e\) are implicitly parametrized by \(\lambda\).

This captures, for example, Vector OLE (with \(\mathbb{G}_{1}=\mathbb{G}_{T}=\mathbb{F}^{n}\) and \(e\colon\mathbb{F}^{n}\times\mathbb{F}\to\mathbb{F}^{n}\) by \(e(\mathbf{u},x)=x\mathbf{u}\)), \(n\)-OLE (with \(\mathbb{G}_{1}=\mathbb{G}_{2}=\mathbb{G}_{T}=\mathbb{F}^{n}\) and \(e\colon\mathbb{F}^{n}\times\mathbb{F}^{n}\to\mathbb{F}^{n}\) by \(e(\mathbf{u},\mathbf{y})=\mathbf{u}\ast\mathbf{v}\) componentwise multiplication), and String OT and \(n\)-OT as special cases for \(\mathbb{F}=\mathbb{F}_{2}\).

**Definition 38**: **(Simple Bilinear Correlation: \(M\)-party).** _For simple bilinear \(2\)-party correlation \(\mathcal{C}_{2}\) specified by \(e\colon\mathbb{G}_{1}\times\mathbb{G}_{2}\to\mathbb{G}_{T}\) we define the corresponding \(M\)-party correlation \(\mathcal{C}_{M}\) by_

\[\mathcal{C}_{M}=\left\{(a_{i},b_{i},c_{i})_{i\in[M]}\left|\begin{array}{c}a_{i }\overset{\sharp}{\leftarrow}\mathbb{G}_{1},b_{i}\overset{\sharp}{\leftarrow }\mathbb{G}_{2}\ \forall i\in[M],\ c_{i}\overset{\sharp}{\leftarrow}\mathbb{G}_{T}\ \forall i\in[M-1],\\ c_{M}=e\left(\sum_{i=1}^{M}a_{i},\sum_{i=1}^{M}b_{i}\right)-\sum_{i=1}^{M-1}c_ {i}\end{array}\right\}\right.\]

_Example 39_: Useful specific examples:

* \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{\sc M-VOLE}}\): Each party holds random \((\mathbf{u}_{i},x_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i})\in\mathbb{F}^{n}\times\mathbb{F}\times\mathbb{F}^ {n}\) \(\text{s.t.}\ (\sum x_{i})\left(\sum\mathbf{u}_{i}\right)=(\sum\mathbf{v}_{i})\)
* \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{\sc M-OLE}}\): Each party holds random \((\mathbf{u}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i},\mathbf{w}_{i})\in\mathbb{F}^{n}\times\mathbb{F}^{n}\times \mathbb{F}^{n}\) \(\text{s.t.}\ (\sum\mathbf{u}_{i})*(\sum\mathbf{v}_{i})=(\sum\mathbf{w}_{i})\ (\text{ componentwise})\)

We consider \(2\)-party PCGs that support the following notion of _programmability_: loosely, that allow a party to "reuse" a piece of his input (either \(a\in\mathbb{G}_{1}\) or \(b\in\mathbb{G}_{2}\)) in multiple instances of the \(2\)-party correlation, while maintaining security.

**Definition 40**: **(Programmability).** _We will say that a PCG \(\mathsf{PCG}=(\mathsf{PCG}.\mathsf{Gen},\mathsf{PCG}.\mathsf{Expand})\) for simple bilinear \(2\)-party correlation \(\mathcal{C}_{2}\) (specified by \(e\colon\mathbb{G}_{1}\times\mathbb{G}_{2}\to\mathbb{G}_{T}\)) support reusable inputs if \(\mathsf{PCG}.\mathsf{Gen}(1^{\lambda})\) takes additional random inputs \(a^{\prime},b^{\prime}\in\{0,1\}^{*}\) such that:_

* _Programmability._ _There exist public efficiently computable functions_ \(f_{a},f_{b}\) _for which_ \[\Pr\left[\begin{array}{c}a^{\prime},b^{\prime}\leftarrow\$,(\mathsf{k}_{0}, \mathsf{k}_{1})\overset{\sharp}{\leftarrow}\mathsf{PCG}.\mathsf{Gen}(1^{ \lambda},a^{\prime},b^{\prime})\\ (a,c)\leftarrow\mathsf{PCG}.\mathsf{Expand}(0,\mathsf{k}_{0}),\\ (b,d)\leftarrow\mathsf{PCG}.\mathsf{Expand}(1,\mathsf{k}_{1})\end{array}: \begin{array}{c}a=f_{a}(a^{\prime})\\ b=f_{b}(b^{\prime})\end{array}\right]\geq 1-\mathsf{negl}(\lambda).\]
* _Security._ _The distributions_ \[\left\{(\mathsf{k}_{1},b^{\prime},f_{a}(a^{\prime}))\left|\begin{array} []{c}(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})\leftarrow\$\\ (\mathsf{k}_{0},\mathsf{k}_{1})\overset{\sharp}{\leftarrow}\mathsf{PCG}. \mathsf{Gen}(1^{\lambda},a^{\prime},b^{\prime})\end{array}\right.\right\} \quad\text{and}\] \[\left\{(\mathsf{k}_{1},b^{\prime},f_{a}(\tilde{a}))\left|\begin{array} []{c}(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})\leftarrow\$,\tilde{a}\leftarrow\$\\ (\mathsf{k}_{0},\mathsf{k}_{1})\overset{\sharp}{\leftarrow}\mathsf{PCG}. \mathsf{Gen}(1^{\lambda},a^{\prime},b^{\prime})\end{array}\right.\right\}\] _are computationally close. A symmetric requirement holds for_ \(b^{\prime},\tilde{b}\)_._

In Appendix G, we present and analyze the following general transformation from any _programmable_\(2\)-party PCG for a simple bilinear correlation to a \(M\)-party PCG for the corresponding multi-party correlation.

**Theorem 41**: **(Multi-party Simple Bilinear PCG).** _Let \(\mathsf{PCG}_{2}=(\mathsf{PCG}_{2}.\mathsf{Gen},\mathsf{PCG}_{2}.\mathsf{Expand})\) be a programmable PCG for simple bilinear \(2\)-party correlation \(\mathcal{C}_{2}\) (specified by \(e\colon\mathbb{G}_{1}\times\mathbb{G}_{2}\to\mathbb{G}_{T}\)) with key sizes \(s_{0}(\lambda),s_{1}(\lambda)\). Then there exists a PCG \(\mathsf{PCG}_{M}=(\mathsf{PCG}_{M}.\mathsf{Gen},\mathsf{PCG}_{M}.\mathsf{Expand})\) for the corresponding \(M\)-party correlation \(\mathcal{C}_{M}\) with the following properties._

* \(\mathsf{PCG}_{M}.\mathsf{Gen}(1^{\lambda})\) _runs_ \(M(M-1)\) _executions of_ \(\mathsf{PCG}_{2}.\mathsf{Gen}\)_; each output key_ \(\mathsf{k}_{i}\)_,_ \(i\in[M]\)_, has size_ \((M-1)(s_{0}(\lambda)+s_{1}(\lambda)+\lambda)\) _bits._
* \(\mathsf{PCG}_{M}.\mathsf{Expand}(i,\mathsf{k}_{i})\) _runs_ \(2(M-1)\) _executions of_ \(\mathsf{PCG}_{2}.\mathsf{Expand}\) _and makes_ \((M-1)\) _evaluations of a pseudorandom generator.__Acknowledgements._ We would like to thank Peter Rindal and Melissa Rossi for helpful discussions and pointers, and the anonymous Crypto 2019 reviewers for their comments.

