We use the above analysis to choose optimal parameters to achieve 80 bits of security against Grobner basis attacks, Gaussian elimination, parity checks, and ISD. The optimal parameters are represented on Table 5.

#### e.2.5 Size of the Seeds.

We estimate below the size of the seeds stored by \(P_{0}\) and \(P_{1}\), using the MPFSS of [13]. For concreteness, we consider an AES-based implementation of the PRGs, and instantiate the elliptic curve with the two candidates described above. The PRG seeds \(\mathsf{k}_{0},\mathsf{k}_{1}\) are \(\lambda\)-bit long, while \(|\boldsymbol{K}_{0}|=|\boldsymbol{K}_{1}|=k\cdot(\lceil\log\ell\rceil\cdot( \lambda+2)+\lambda+\lceil\log q\rceil)\) with \(\ell=\alpha\cdot n\). Each \(\boldsymbol{c}_{i}\) for \(i=1,2\) is of size \(2\cdot\sqrt{\ell}\cdot|\mathbb{G}_{i}|\). Therefore:

