#### e.2.6 Efficiency of Expand.

We now estimate the computational cost of each step of the Expand procedure. In this section, we assume that the column-weight \(d\) of the parity-check matrix of \(N\) is upper bounded by \(10\), which was shown to be reasonable choice in [1]. With this parameters, computing the map \(\mathbf{m}\mapsto N\cdot\mathbf{m}\) requires \(10\cdot(2\alpha-1)n\) XORs using the linear-time algorithm of [1, 12] (see e.g. [1]). For each of the two parallel executions of Expand, the parties do the following (we ignore some costs which are several orders of magnitude smaller than all other costs, e.g. computing \(\mathbf{x}\gets N\cdot\mathbf{d}_{0}\) and \(\mathbf{y}\gets N\cdot\mathbf{d}_{1}\)):

1. Both parties execute \(4\) instances of MPFSS.FullEval.
2. \(P_{0}\) computes \(4\ell\) pairings (to build \(\mathbf{c}_{t}\) from \(\mathbf{c}_{1},\mathbf{c}_{2}\)).
3. \(P_{1}\) computes \(9\ell\) exponentiations over \(\mathbb{G}_{t}\) (to build \(\mathbf{c}_{t}\) from \((\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b},\mathbf{r}_{1},\mathbf{r}_{2})\)).
4. Both parties compute \(40\cdot(2\alpha-1)n\) multiplications over \(\mathbb{G}_{t}\) (to build \((c^{\prime}_{i})_{i\leq n}\) from \(\mathbf{c}_{t}\) and \(N\); the XORs are evaluated as homomorphic additions over \(\mathbb{Z}_{q}\), and each such homomorphic addition requires the term-by-term product of two ciphertexts, hence \(4\) multiplications over \(\mathbb{G}_{t}\)).
5. The parties execute \(n\) distributed discrete logarithms (the Convert algorithm). The efficiency of this step depends on the average size of the values \(z_{i}\) such that \(\mathsf{Pair}(c^{\prime}_{i},(\mathsf{sk}_{t}\cdot d^{\prime}_{i}))=\{g^{z_{ i}}_{t}\}\). Concretely, each \(z_{i}\) is the product of the \(i\)'th coordinates of \(N\cdot\mathbf{d}_{0}\) and \(N\cdot\mathbf{d}_{1}\). Assuming that \(N\) has average row-weight \(\ell/2\), and since \(\mathbf{d}_{0}\) has \(k\) non-zero entries (all equal to \(1\)) and \(\mathbf{d}_{1}=\mathbf{a}\otimes\mathbf{b}\) has \(k^{2}\) non-zero entries (all equal to \(1\)), the average size of \(z_{i}\) is \(k^{3}/4\). Using the optimized DDLOG of [12] with average payload \(k^{3}/4\), the failure probability \(\delta\) of the output is approximately \(\delta=B(T)\cdot k^{3}/(4\cdot T^{2})\), where \(T\) is the number of multiplications over \(\mathbb{G}_{t}\) performed by each party, and \(B(T)\) is a value below \(2^{10.4}\) (optimal values of \(B\) for various \(T\) are given in Table 1 of [12]). We represent on Table 7 the failure \(\delta\) obtained for various output lengths \(n\) and number of multiplications \(T\), using the values \(B(T)\) from Table 1 of [12].

